\begin{align*}
z + \frac{1}{z} = -1, \quad \text{from which we get}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\left(z + \frac{1}{z}\right)^2 &= (-1)^2 = 1, \\
\left(z^2 + \frac{1}{z^2}\right)^2 &= (z + \frac{1}{z})^2 - 2 = -1, \\
\left(z^3 + \frac{1}{z^3}\right)^2 &= (z^2 + \frac{1}{z^2})^2 - 2 = (-1)^2 - 2 = -1, \\
\left(z^4 + \frac{1}{z^4}\right)^2 &= \left[(z^2 + \frac{1}{z^2})(z + \frac{1}{z})\right]^2 = (-1)^2(-1)^2 = 1, \\
\left(z^5 + \frac{1}{z^5}\right)^2 &= \left(z^4 + \frac{1}{z^4}\right)\left(z + \frac{1}{z}\right) = 1(-1) = -1, \\
\left(z^6 + \frac{1}{z^6}\right)^2 &= \left(z^5 + \frac{1}{z^5}\right)\left(z + \frac{1}{z}\right) = (-1)(-1) = 1. \\
\end{align*}.
Thus, because the sequence $\left(z^n + \frac{1}{z^n}\right)^2$ has period 6, the required sum is $24 \cdot 1 + 22 \cdot (-1)$ $= \boxed{-10}.$</think> <answer>$\boxed{-10}$</answer>

